<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <!--
      docs/plan/03 § Providers — the fifth project: "the shared provider conformance suite,
      parameterised".

      ⚠ IT CONTAINS NO ASSERTIONS, AND THIS IS THE PROJECT THAT TESTS THAT CLAIM. The sample's
      .Conformance project asserted that a provider costs one ProviderConformanceCase and two class
      declarations; a claim demonstrated once by its own author is a claim about one provider. This is
      the second, written against a type with two rendered objects rather than one, and it is still
      one case object and two class declarations — test/CyberCloud.Conformance was not touched.

      ⚠ THIS PROJECT IS ALSO THE SECOND SUITE THE Labels ARCHITECTURE GATE COUNTS. Build.Architecture
      § LabelsGate runs EveryAppliedObjectCarriesTheSevenMandatoryLabelsAndBothAnnotations in every
      *.Conformance project under src/Providers, and reported "1 provider conformance suite(s)" until
      this one existed. It is the first suite in which the seven labels have to survive onto a
      CUSTOM RESOURCE rather than a core-group ConfigMap.

      ⚠ IT REFERENCES A PROJECT UNDER test/, which docs/plan/03 § test/ intends: CyberCloud.Conformance
      is "the shared provider suite (parameterised, REFERENCED BY PROVIDERS)". docs/plan/03 § Assembly
      graph rules is about the shipped graph and excludes test projects by construction.
    -->

    <ItemGroup>
        <PackageReference Include="xunit.v3"/>
        <PackageReference Include="Shouldly"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup Label="Global usings">
        <Using Include="Xunit"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <ProjectReference Include="../CyberCloud.Providers.DBforPostgreSQL/CyberCloud.Providers.DBforPostgreSQL.csproj"/>
        <ProjectReference Include="../../../../test/CyberCloud.Conformance/CyberCloud.Conformance.csproj"/>
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
